\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b08e89e3902ba9360f9961f50e9f3f1b8ba1a959fe0de04a87724d72ff237043"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a hot drink decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the cube of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the drink $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a hot drink **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **cube of the temperature** $T.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *cube of the temperature.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b08e89e3902ba9360f9961f50e9f3f1b8ba1a959fe0de04a87724d72ff237043"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{\\sqrt[3]{T}},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{T},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-79306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intensity $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the intensity of the light source $I?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **intensity** $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance** $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *intensity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the distance.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the intensity *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b08e89e3902ba9360f9961f50e9f3f1b8ba1a959fe0de04a87724d72ff237043"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=-k{I}^{2},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{I}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=k{d}^{2},\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d7fce0fc900bfeddebcb41d2c55053505aab53f2373479582fcfe5d4590064"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883"}] "usage" {"q-143078" [] "e-522" [] "e-521" [] "q-143029" [] "q-51247" [] "e-10073" [] "q-143081" [] "q-38593" [] "q-143094" [] "e-10071" [] "q-353449" [] "q-38310" [] "q-143037" [] "q-38589" []} "basis" 1254 "retractions" [[17592186129576 :question/problem "For the first year of a newborn's life, its height $H$ increases at a rate that is proportional to the time, in months, since birth.\n\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height of the baby at time $t$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127251 17592186127252 17592186127253 17592186127254 17592186127255 17592186128289 17592186128290 17592186128291 17592186128292 17592186128293 17592186281719 17592186281720 17592186281721 17592186281722 17592186281723 17592186281726 17592186281727 17592186281728 17592186281729 17592186281730 17592186281754 17592186281755 17592186281756 17592186281757 17592186281758 17592186281761 17592186281762 17592186281763 17592186281764 17592186281765 17592186281768 17592186281769 17592186281770 17592186281771 17592186281772 17592186281779 17592186281789 17592186281790 17592186281791 17592186281792 17592186281793 17592186281803 17592186281804 17592186281805 17592186281806 17592186281807] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127250 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127250 :answer-field/id] [17592186127250 :answer-field/key] [17592186127250 :answer-field/type] [17592186128288 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128288 :answer-field/id] [17592186128288 :answer-field/key] 